          "id": "illinois-overlapping-wildcard-and-stated-limits",
          "hypothesis_ids": [
            "EC",
            "EV"
          ],
          "guidance": {
            "by_task": [
              {
                "tasks": [
                  "EC"
                ],
                "when": "ILLINOIS is allowable and the debtor owns personal property, including property expressly identified as a tool of the debtor's trade.",
                "procedure": "Evaluate 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) independently for every item of personal property, including when subsection (a), (c), (d), or (e) also applies. Section (a) covers necessary wearing apparel, (c) covers a motor vehicle, (d) covers an implement, professional book, or tool expressly used in the debtor's trade, and (e) covers a professionally prescribed health aid. A value above a limit does not eliminate an applicable alternative.",
                "avoid_when": "Do not apply § 12-1001(b) to real property. Do not classify jewelry or a watch as necessary wearing apparel, or a device that displays pictures as a family picture. Require express trade-use facts for subsection (d), and do not classify business machinery as a motor vehicle merely because it can move."
              },
              {
                "tasks": [
                  "EV"
                ],
                "when": "ILLINOIS is allowable and the debtor owns personal property or an occupied homestead.",
                "procedure": "Determine eligibility before amounts. For every item of personal property, report § 12-1001(b) independently at the lesser of value and $4,000 for one debtor or $8,000 for jointly owned property of two debtors, including an expressly identified trade tool and property also covered by (a), (c), (d), or (e). Report subsection (c) for a motor vehicle at the lesser of value and $2,400 without doubling. Report subsection (d) for an expressly identified trade tool at the lesser of value and $1,500 for one debtor or $3,000 for jointly owned property of two debtors. Subsections (a) and (e) use asset value. For an equal-half jointly owned occupied homestead, § 12-901 gives a $15,000 proportionate-share claim, not one combined $30,000 claim.",
                "avoid_when": "Do not apply personal-property subsections to real property, including burial realty or a homestead. Do not use subsection (d) without express trade-use facts, subsection (c) for business machinery that is not a motor vehicle, or subsection (a) for jewelry, a watch, or a device merely capable of displaying family pictures. Do not treat any dollar ceiling as an eligibility test."
              }
            ],
            "operators": [],
            "jurisdiction": "ILLINOIS"
          }
        },
        "source_case_ids": [
          195,
          279,
          281,
          283,
          285,
          292
        ],
        "rationale": "The parent repeatedly omitted § 12-1001(b) from above-cap vehicles and expressly identified trade tools, omitted subsection (d) because trade-property value exceeded its limit, and misclassified trade machinery under subsection (c). It also applied the personal-property wildcard to real property and doubled the joint homestead amount. These sources support separate corrections for missing overlapping alternatives, category boundaries, and claim amounts while preserving the express-facts requirement for trade tools."
      }
